To execute two different prepared statements in a single batch, you can make use of transactions. By wrapping the statements in a transaction, you ensure that they are executed together, and if any of the statements fail, the changes are rolled back.

Here's an updated version of your code that uses transactions to execute the statements in a single batch:

```java
import java.lang.ClassNotFoundException;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.PreparedStatement;
import java.sql.SQLException;
import java.sql.DriverManager;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Connection connection = null;
        PreparedStatement preparedStatementWithdraw = null;
        PreparedStatement preparedStatementDeposit = null;

        try {
            Class.forName("com.mysql.jdbc.Driver");
            connection = DriverManager.getConnection("jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/youtube", "root", "root");
            connection.setAutoCommit(false); // Enable manual control of transactions

            preparedStatementWithdraw = withdrawFromChecking(connection, preparedStatementWithdraw, new BigDecimal(100), 1);
            preparedStatementDeposit = depositIntoSaving(connection, preparedStatementDeposit, new BigDecimal(300), 1);

            preparedStatementWithdraw.executeBatch();
            preparedStatementDeposit.executeBatch();
            connection.commit(); // Commit the changes

            System.out.println("Account Modified!");
        } catch(ClassNotFoundException error) {
            System.out.println("Error: " + error.getMessage());
        } catch(SQLException error) {
            System.out.println("Error: " + error.getMessage());
            if(connection != null) {
                try {
                    connection.rollback(); // Rollback the changes if there is an error
                } catch (SQLException rollbackError) {
                    System.out.println("Rollback Error: " + rollbackError.getMessage());
                }
            }
        } finally {
            if(connection != null) try{connection.close();} catch(SQLException error) {}
            if(preparedStatementDeposit != null) try{preparedStatementDeposit.close();} catch(SQLException error) {}
        }
    }

    public static PreparedStatement withdrawFromChecking(Connection connection, PreparedStatement preparedStatement, BigDecimal balance, int id) throws SQLException {
        preparedStatement = connection.prepareStatement("UPDATE bankAccount SET checkingBalance = checkingBalance - ? WHERE id = ?");
        preparedStatement.setBigDecimal(1, balance);
        preparedStatement.setInt(2, id);
        preparedStatement.addBatch();

        return preparedStatement;
    }

    public static PreparedStatement depositIntoSaving(Connection connection, PreparedStatement preparedStatement, BigDecimal balance, int id) throws SQLException {
        preparedStatement = connection.prepareStatement("UPDATE bankAccount SET savingBalance = savingBalance + ? WHERE id = ?");
        preparedStatement.setBigDecimal(1, balance);
        preparedStatement.setInt(2, id);
        preparedStatement.addBatch();

        return preparedStatement;
    }
}
```

In this updated code:

1. The `connection.setAutoCommit(false)` statement is added to disable automatic commit. This allows manual control over transactions.

2. After executing the statements, `preparedStatementWithdraw.executeBatch()` and `preparedStatementDeposit.executeBatch()` are called to execute the prepared statements in a single batch.

3. If there is no exception, `connection.commit()` is called to commit the changes made by the statements.

4. In case of an exception, `connection.rollback()` is called in the catch block to rollback the changes made by the statements.

By using the `connection.setAutoCommit(false)`, `connection.commit()`, and `connection.rollback()` methods, you can ensure that both statements are executed as a single batch within a transaction.

